  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1101 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nWe could get some isolated showers or thunderstorms develop as\nearly as Tuesday evening as the longwave trough axis and\nassociated surface front push east into eastern Arkansas/western\nMississippi. Better chances for rain look to develop overnight \ninto Wednesday morning over northwestern Alabama. This still looks\nto be a heavy rainfall event given several aspects of the \nevolution of this storm system. Overall surface based instability \nwill be very hard to get and 0-6 km bulk shear does not look quite\nas strong as a few days ago. However, PWATS will be high for this\ntime of year (1.5 to 1.65 inches). Also, during the day on \nWednesday as the front moves closer to the area, upper level \ndiffluence will be strong west of the I-65 corridor and we will be\nin the right entrance region of the upper level jet. Also, a 40 \nknot 850 jet will be over the area as well. This all points to \nvery strong/deep lift that will be in place, maybe primarily over \nareas west of the I-65 corridor into northeastern Mississippi. One\nto three inches of rainfall with locally up to 4 inches looks \npossible in those areas. It does look like there will be a decent \ndrop off in rainfall totals near and east of the I-65 corridor \n(maybe not even over 1 inch). A few models are hinting that higher\namounts over 5 inches could be possible in NW Alabama through \nWednesday evening. One of the main reasons this setup may occur is\nthe stoutness of the upper ridge just off the coast of the \nCarolinas and Virginia. The upper low butts heads with this \nfeature during that period before finally moving northward over it\nlate Wednesday night into Thursday, taking precipitation \nnorthward with it. Wednesday should be cooler again with clouds \nand precipitation expected. Highs will only reach the upper 60s \nto lower 70s.\n\nNot much change into Friday with lingering cloud cover as the \nupper low continues to move northeast into the central Great Lakes\nregion.",
